\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.1}The complexity ladder}{26}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:scenarios}{{5.1}{26}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The complexity ladder. Each panel adds one mechanism to the tier before it; the series shown are the Leasehold share, the farm-size Gini, the landless share and aggregate output. A dynamic first appearing at tier $n$ is attributable to the mechanism tier $n$ introduces, which is what a single all-mechanisms run cannot establish.}}{26}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ladder}{{2}{26}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.2}RQ\ref {rq:improvement}: competitive tenancy or secure property}{27}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-rq1}{{5.2}{27}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Improving disposition $\iota _j^{T}(t)$ by tenure state, Leasehold against Freehold on the same land in the same runs. Brenner's landlord--tenant symbiosis predicts Leasehold leads; Allen's yeoman predicts Freehold does. The middle panel repeats the comparison with \texttt  {freehold\_shadow\_rent} disabled, which removes the competitive yardstick from Freehold entirely; the right-hand panel varies the initial tenure composition instead.}}{27}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq1-tenure}{{3}{27}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Capital, output per parcel and wealth per tenant by tenure state in the baseline arm, where Freehold feels the competitive benchmark. The Freehold series is the one that accumulates; the oscillation in all three is the ecological cycle of \S  \ref {sec:exogenous} propagating through investment.}}{28}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq1-accum}{{4}{28}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The within-tenure half of RQ\ref {rq:improvement}: every converted parcel aligned on its own conversion date, against parcels that never converted (flat reference). Reported with the occupant-continuity diagnostic, since a conversion that replaces the sitting tenant records a change of occupant rather than a change of disposition (\S  \ref {sec:vacancy}).}}{28}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq1-event}{{5}{28}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.3}RQ\ref {rq:spread}: spreading or simultaneous}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-rq2}{{5.3}{29}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The semivariogram of first-conversion time: half the mean squared difference in conversion time between pairs of parcels, against the lattice distance separating them, normalised by the total variance. No origin is assumed. A curve rising from a low value at short distance is a spreading front, and the distance at which it flattens is the spatial scale of the process. A flat line at unity throughout is simultaneity: parcels one cell apart convert as differently as parcels a country apart.}}{29}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq2-lag}{{6}{29}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Share of county land under Leasehold, averaged over seeds, at six points through the run. If the transition travelled, the sequence would show a front; it shows uniform deepening instead.}}{30}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq2-film}{{7}{30}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Both transmission channels disabled, differenced against the baseline parcel by parcel on matching seeds. A transmission mechanism with spatial content would leave a spatially structured difference; this one leaves a uniform level shift.}}{31}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq2-diff}{{8}{31}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.4}RQ\ref {rq:alliance}: necessary, sufficient or permissive}{32}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-rq3}{{5.4}{32}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The $2\times 2$ of RQ\ref {rq:alliance}: England ($\theta = 2$) and France ($\theta = 6$) crossed with the class-conflict mechanism enabled and disabled ($\alpha _1 = 0$, $\varphi = 0$). The England outcome surviving the disabled column would make the political condition sufficient on its own, which is Bois's charge; $\theta $ merely rescaling a transition that occurs in both columns would make it permissive.}}{32}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq3}{{9}{32}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Conflict intensity $\alpha _1$ against institutional resistance $\theta $, 64 seeds in every cell. The left panel is the probability that the transition completes, with the frontier at $P = 0.5$ drawn on it; a frontier parallel to an axis would make that axis redundant, and a diagonal one means the two conditions trade off at the slope shown.}}{33}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq3-surface}{{10}{33}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.5}RQ\ref {rq:security}: how secure custom could have been}{33}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-rq4}{{5.5}{33}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Final Leasehold and Customary shares over the sweep of the arbitrary-fine rate $\varphi $, the terms-reopening hazard $\varpi $ and institutional resistance $\theta $, each varied alone. The dashed line is the completion criterion, a Leasehold majority.}}{33}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq4}{{11}{33}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.6}RQ\ref {rq:dispossession}: necessity of dispossession}{34}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-rq5}{{5.6}{34}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Conversion routed through succession vacancies and fine escalation alone, with the crisis channel damped ($\eta _1 = \eta _2 = 0$, eviction suspended), against the full baseline and against each channel removed singly.}}{34}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq5}{{12}{34}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.7}RQ\ref {rq:ecology}: ecology or class structure}{35}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-rq6}{{5.7}{35}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The confound stated directly. Left: the ALC-derived carrying capacity $\bar  \phi $ that the lattice is built from. Right: mean period of first conversion against that carrying capacity, binned by decile of $\bar  \phi $.}}{35}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq6}{{13}{35}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Uniform fertility differenced against ALC regional fertility, parcel by parcel on matching seeds. An ecological driver of the transition would leave a regionally organised difference; this one leaves speckle.}}{36}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rq6-diff}{{14}{36}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {5.8}Claims about the model}{37}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:res-checks}{{5.8}{37}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces The diagnostics of \S  \ref {sec:rq-model}: aggregate tenure composition, the attribution of the farm-size Gini to engrossment rather than turnover, the share of parcels no household can take up, and the cross-seed relation between tenure outcomes and population. A failure in any panel is a defect to repair before the results above can be read.}}{37}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:checks}{{15}{37}{}{}{}}
